 # Tetrahedral Mesh Representation
 
-Also called: Tetrahedral Volume, Unstructured Tetrahedral Grid
+Also called: Cell Complexes, Simplicial Complexes, Unstructured Mesh
 
 ## Overview
-Tetrahedral Mesh Representation encodes volumetric data using a collection of tetrahedral elements defined by arbitrary vertex positions, enabling adaptive resolution and complex topology that regular grids cannot support. It differs from voxel-based methods by eliminating aliasing through deformable vertices and from surface meshes by providing a true volumetric interior, allowing for accurate density integration and interior feature representation.
+Tetrahedral Mesh Representation explicitly defines both the geometry (vertex positions) and micro-topology (connectivity of vertices into tetrahedra) of a volume, allowing for arbitrary, non-regular domain partitioning [S1, unofficial]. It solves the limitation of regular grids by adapting resolution locally to feature density or error metrics, enabling high-fidelity representation of complex geometries and internal structures that do not align with voxel axes [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings [Adaptive Octree Construction with 2:1 Balance and Surface Propagation](830-adaptive-octree-construction-with-2-1-balance-and-surface-pr.md) and [Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) Data Structures](5344-adaptive-mesh-refinement-amr-data-structures.md) by using unstructured simplicial elements (tetrahedra) rather than hierarchical box-based cells, and from [Point Cloud Density Evaluation](1743-point-cloud-density-evaluation.md) by providing explicit volumetric connectivity rather than implicit density fields.
 
