+### Piecewise-Linear Topology Representation
+Represents a geometric model as a tuple $(K, V)$, where $K$ is a combinatorial simplicial complex specifying connectivity and $V$ is a set of vertex positions in $\mathbb{R}^n$ [S1, unofficial]. An $n$-simplex is defined by $n+1$ linearly independent points $v_0, ..., v_n$, and a simplex spanned by a subset of vertices is a face [S1, unofficial]. A cell complex is a finite collection of cells satisfying: 1. If $B, C \notin K$ and $B < C$, then $B \notin K$. 2. If $B, C \notin K$ and $B \neq C$, then $B \cap C$ is a face of both $B$ and $C$ [S1, unofficial]. An $n$-manifold is a polyhedron where each point has a neighborhood homeomorphic to an open set in $\mathbb{R}^n$ [S1, unofficial]. A pseudo-manifold is a regular complex where: 1. Every $(n-1)$-face is a face of some $n$-cell. 2. Every $n$-cell is the face of exactly two $n$-cells. 3. Any two $n$-cells can be connected by a sequence of $n$-cells sharing $(n-1)$-faces [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of transforming volume data into tetrahedral pieces for rendering was discussed by Shirley and Tuchman [ST93], who allocated transparency and color values to each tetrahedron based on its position and distance from the viewer, subsequently scan-converting the pieces using a painters algorithm [S1, unofficial]. Another projection method described by Drebin et al. [DCH88] transforms volume data so that 'rays' can be projected into the volume and onto the image plane using a scan-line method, achievable at any angle via a sequence of four transformations using Euler angles [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of piecewise-linear topology and simplicial complexes forms the mathematical foundation for unstructured meshes [S1, unofficial]. Simplicial complexes are the principal building blocks, comprising simplices (vertices, edges, triangles, tetrahedra) and cells [S1, unofficial]. The most general representation, the unstructured mesh, explicitly specifies both geometry and micro-topology using $n$-tuples of indices into a vertex list to form $n$-cells [S1, unofficial]. While structured meshes simplify compression by requiring only geometric information, unstructured meshes are necessary for problems not defined on regular grids, such as those in computational fluid dynamics (CFD) and finite element analysis (FEA) [S1, unofficial]. Memory consumption for an unstructured mesh with 32-bit floats for vertices and 32-bit integers for cells is approximately 32 bits/vertex, $3 \log_2(v)$ bits/triangle for triangle incidence, and $4 \log_2(v)$ bits/tetrahedron for tetrahedron incidence, where $v$ is the total number of vertices [S1, unofficial]. Early rendering approaches using tetrahedral meshes involved transforming volume data into tetrahedra pieces and scan converting them using a painters algorithm [S2, unofficial].
 
